            <ArticleTitle>Left anterior descending coronary artery spasm and &quot;accordion effect&quot; mimicking coronary artery dissection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>309-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.medici.2014.10.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1010-660X(14)00090-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present an illustrative case report of the mid-vessel left anterior descending (LAD) coronary artery spasm, which has been enhanced by straightening of the tortuous LAD segment with a guidewire (&quot;accordion effect&quot;) thus resistant to intracoronary nitroglycerine and mimicking a coronary artery dissection. Dynamic appearance of this iatrogenic pseudo-lesion upon administration of intracoronary nitrates and recognition of these angiographic phenomena prevented from unnecessary further intervention.</AbstractText>
